\title{Resolution, Restrictions, and Cutting Planes:\\
A Verified Account of Pigeonhole Hardness and Its Separation}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-29}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained development of the two propositional proof systems that
anchor modern proof complexity --- \emph{resolution} and \emph{cutting planes} ---
and use them to dissect the pigeonhole principle, the canonical hard family of the
field. We develop resolution as a clause calculus with a single inference rule
and prove its soundness end-to-end: the resolvent of two satisfied clauses is
satisfied, every derivable clause is a semantic consequence, and a formula
admitting a refutation is unsatisfiable. We encode the pigeonhole principle
$\PHP_n$ for $n+1$ pigeons and $n$ holes as a CNF and prove it unsatisfiable by
extracting an injection $\{1,\dots,n+1\}\to\{1,\dots,n\}$ from any satisfying
assignment. We then build the structural engine of resolution lower bounds:
\emph{weakening}, the \emph{nonexistence of derivations from the empty formula},
and --- centrally --- the \emph{restriction operator} together with its exact
semantic invariant: a free assignment satisfies a restricted formula if and only
if the glued assignment satisfies the original. From this we derive \emph{hardness
preservation} and apply it to show that $\PHP_n$ is hard \emph{robustly}: every
partial assignment leaves it unsatisfiable. Finally we develop cutting planes
--- integer-linear inequalities with the addition and Chv\'atal--Gomory rounding
rules, both proved sound --- and exhibit the \emph{linear counting refutation} of
the pigeonhole principle, the constructive half of the exponential separation
between cutting planes and resolution. Throughout we situate the results against
Haken's exponential lower bound and against SAT-solver practice.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A \emph{satisfiability solver} answers, for a propositional formula, whether some
assignment makes it true. When the answer is ``yes,'' the solver returns a witness
that anyone can check. When the answer is ``no,'' it must return a \emph{certificate
of unsatisfiability} --- a formal proof, in a fixed proof system, that no
assignment works. \emph{Proof complexity} studies the size of such certificates.

The field's organizing benchmark is the \emph{pigeonhole principle}: $n+1$ pigeons
cannot be placed into $n$ holes without a collision. As a propositional formula it
is unsatisfiable for a transparent, global counting reason, yet it has been the
proving ground for the deepest lower bounds in the area. Two systems frame the
theory: \emph{resolution}, the local clause-elimination calculus underlying
essentially all conflict-driven SAT solvers (Haken (1985): refutations of the
pigeonhole principle have size exponential in $n$); and \emph{cutting planes}, an
arithmetic system that refutes the pigeonhole principle in a linear number of
steps and is therefore exponentially separated from resolution.

Our contributions are: (1) a soundness pipeline for resolution
(Section~\ref{sec:res}); (2) a faithful CNF encoding of the pigeonhole principle
and a proof of its unsatisfiability (Section~\ref{sec:php}); (3) the algebra of
restrictions --- weakening, the empty-formula fact, the exact restriction
invariant, hardness preservation, and robust pigeonhole hardness
(Section~\ref{sec:restr}); and (4) soundness of the cutting-planes rules and the
linear counting refutation, the constructive side of the separation
(Section~\ref{sec:cp}).

\section{Preliminaries: literals, clauses, and CNF}

Fix a type $V$ of propositional variables. An \emph{assignment} is a function
$a : V \to \{\true,\false\}$.

\begin{definition}[Literal]
A \emph{literal} is a pair $\ell = (v,b)$ of a variable $v\in V$ and polarity
$b\in\{\true,\false\}$; we write $v$ for $(v,\true)$ and $\neg v$ for $(v,\false)$.
A literal is evaluated by $\eval_a(\ell) = [\,a(\ell.v)=\ell.\mathrm{pos}\,]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Clause]
A \emph{clause} $C$ is a finite list of literals, read disjunctively, satisfied by
$a$ (written $a\models C$) iff $\exists\,\ell\in C,\ \eval_a(\ell)=\true$. The
\emph{empty clause} $\square$ is satisfied by no assignment and serves as $\bot$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[CNF]
A \emph{CNF formula} $F$ is a finite list of clauses, read conjunctively,
satisfied by $a$ (written $a\models F$) iff $\forall\,C\in F,\ a\models C$. $F$ is
\emph{satisfiable} if some $a$ satisfies it, \emph{unsatisfiable} otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:empty}
For every assignment $a$, $a\not\models\square$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The empty clause has no literal to witness satisfaction.
\end{proof}

\section{The resolution proof system}\label{sec:res}

Assume $V$ has decidable equality.

\begin{definition}[Resolvent]
The \emph{resolvent} of $C_1,C_2$ on pivot $p$ is
\[
\res(C_1,C_2,p) = \big(C_1 \text{ with } (p,\true) \text{ removed}\big)
 \mathbin{+\!\!+} \big(C_2 \text{ with } (p,\false) \text{ removed}\big).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of the resolution rule]\label{thm:res-sound}
If $a\models C_1$ and $a\models C_2$, then $a\models\res(C_1,C_2,p)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Case split on $a(p)$. If $a(p)=\true$, the literal $(p,\false)$ of $C_2$ is false,
so $C_2$'s witness is a different literal, which survives the filter into the
second part of the resolvent. If $a(p)=\false$, the symmetric argument uses $C_1$.
No assumption that the pivot occurs in either parent is needed.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Derivability]
The clauses \emph{derivable} from $F$ ($F\vdash C$) are generated by: (base) $C\in
F \Rightarrow F\vdash C$; (res) $F\vdash C_1$ and $F\vdash C_2 \Rightarrow F\vdash
\res(C_1,C_2,p)$ for any $p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of derivation]\label{thm:der-sound}
If $F\vdash C$, then $\forall a,\ a\models F \Rightarrow a\models C$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on the derivation; the base case is immediate and the inductive case is
Theorem~\ref{thm:res-sound} applied to the induction hypotheses.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Refutation]
A \emph{resolution refutation} of $F$ is a derivation $F\vdash\square$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of refutations]\label{thm:ref-sound}
If $F$ has a resolution refutation, then $F$ is unsatisfiable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $a\models F$, then $a\models\square$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:der-sound},
contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:empty}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-vacuity]
The formula $\{x\}\land\{\neg x\}$ satisfies $\res([x],[\neg x],x)=\square$, hence
admits a one-step refutation and is unsatisfiable.
\end{theorem}

\section{The pigeonhole principle as a CNF}\label{sec:php}

\begin{definition}[Pigeonhole variables and clauses]
Set $\PVar_n = \{0,\dots,n\}\times\{0,\dots,n-1\}$; $x_{p,h}$ means ``pigeon $p$ is
in hole $h$.'' The \emph{pigeon clause} for $p$ is $\bigvee_h x_{p,h}$; the
\emph{hole clause} for hole $h$ and distinct ordered pigeons $p_1\neq p_2$ is
$\neg x_{p_1,h}\lor\neg x_{p_2,h}$. $\PHP_n$ is the conjunction of all such clauses.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Unsatisfiability of the pigeonhole principle]\label{thm:php-unsat}
$\PHP_n$ is unsatisfiable for every $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A satisfying $a$ yields, via the pigeon clauses, a function $f$ from pigeons to
occupied holes. The hole clauses force $f$ injective: if $f(p_1)=f(p_2)$ for
$p_1\neq p_2$, the clause $\neg x_{p_1,f(p_1)}\lor\neg x_{p_2,f(p_1)}$ would be
violated. But an injection $\{1,\dots,n+1\}\to\{1,\dots,n\}$ is impossible since
$n<n+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Any resolution refutation of $\PHP_n$ is a correct certificate of its
unsatisfiability; whether a short one exists is what Haken's theorem rules out.
\end{corollary}

\section{Restrictions and the engine of lower bounds}\label{sec:restr}

\begin{theorem}[Weakening]\label{thm:weak}
If $F\subseteq G$, then $F\vdash C \Rightarrow G\vdash C$; in particular a
refutation of $F$ is a refutation of $G$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on the derivation: base clauses and resolution steps are preserved.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The empty formula proves nothing]
$\varnothing\not\vdash C$ for every clause $C$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on the derivation: no base clause exists and every step needs a prior
clause, so no derivation exists.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Restriction, gluing, restricting]
A \emph{restriction} is $\rho:V\to\{\true,\false,\star\}$ ($\star$ = free).
Gluing: $\subst(\rho,a)(v)=\rho(v)$ if fixed, else $a(v)$. A clause is
\emph{killed} if a literal is fixed to its own polarity; the clause restriction
$C\restr\rho$ keeps the literals on free variables (literals fixed to $\false$ are
\emph{deleted}); the CNF restriction $F\restr\rho$ drops killed clauses and
restricts the survivors.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Restriction invariance]\label{thm:restr-inv}
For every $\rho$, $F$, and free assignment $a$,
\[
a \models F\restr\rho \iff \subst(\rho,a)\models F.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A literal-level case split on whether $\rho$ fixes each variable. For
$(\Leftarrow)$: a surviving restricted clause comes from a clause satisfied by
$\subst(\rho,a)$ via a literal $\ell$ that, being neither a killing literal nor
fixed to the opposite value, must be free and hence survives, still true under
$a$. For $(\Rightarrow)$: each $C\in F$ is either killed (a fixing literal
satisfies it) or survives (its restricted witness lifts back); the delicate case
is a literal fixed to $\false$, which is deleted, not killing, and can never have
been the witness because $\subst$ agrees with the fixed value there. The
equivalence is \emph{exact}: no error term.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hardness preservation]\label{thm:hard}
If $F$ is unsatisfiable, then so is $F\restr\rho$ for every $\rho$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $a\models F\restr\rho$, then $\subst(\rho,a)\models F$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:restr-inv}, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Robust pigeonhole hardness]
For every $n$ and every restriction $\rho$, $\PHP_n\restr\rho$ is unsatisfiable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:hard} applied to Theorem~\ref{thm:php-unsat}.
\end{proof}

\section{Cutting planes and the separation}\label{sec:cp}

We encode a constraint as $\sum_i c_i x_i \ge d$ over integer points
$x:\iota\to\mathbb{Z}$.

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of addition]
For finite $s$, if $d_1\le\sum_{i\in s} c_1(i)x(i)$ and
$d_2\le\sum_{i\in s} c_2(i)x(i)$, then
$d_1+d_2\le\sum_{i\in s}(c_1(i)+c_2(i))x(i)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Split the sum and add the hypotheses.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of Chv\'atal--Gomory rounding]
Let $k>0$ divide every $c(i)$, $i\in s$, and $d\le\sum_{i\in s} c(i)x(i)$. Then
$\lceil d/k\rceil \le \sum_{i\in s}(c(i)/k)x(i)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $k\mid c(i)$, $\sum c(i)x(i)=k\,S$ with $S=\sum(c(i)/k)x(i)\in\mathbb{Z}$, so
$d\le kS$ gives $d/k\le S$; as $S$ is an integer, $\lceil d/k\rceil\le S$. The
rounding is where integrality is used.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The counting refutation of the pigeonhole principle]
\label{thm:count}
Let $x:\PVar_n\to\mathbb{Z}$ satisfy $\sum_h x(p,h)\ge1$ for all $p$ and
$\sum_p x(p,h)\le1$ for all $h$. Then a contradiction follows: summing pigeon
bounds gives $n+1\le\sum_p\sum_h x(p,h)$, while summing hole bounds and exchanging
order gives $\sum_p\sum_h x(p,h)=\sum_h\sum_p x(p,h)\le n$, whence $n+1\le n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The lower bound is $\sum_p 1\le\sum_p\sum_h x(p,h)$ by monotonicity of sums; the
upper bound uses commutativity of double summation and $\sum_h 1=n$. The chain
contradicts $n+1>n$.
\end{proof}

This refutation uses only the row/column constraints --- no hidden $0\le x\le1$
assumption --- and consists of $O(n)$ steps. It is the constructive heart of the
separation.

\begin{theorem}[Separation, informal]
The pigeonhole principle has cutting-planes refutations of size $O(n)$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:count}) but, by Haken's theorem, only resolution refutations of
size $2^{\Omega(n)}$. Hence cutting planes is exponentially stronger than
resolution on this family.
\end{theorem}

\section{Relationship to Haken's theorem and SAT solving}

Theorem~\ref{thm:php-unsat} establishes the precondition for the lower-bound
question and Theorem~\ref{thm:ref-sound} makes any refutation a trustworthy
certificate, so ``how large must a refutation be?'' is well-posed; Haken's theorem
answers $2^{\Omega(n)}$. The restriction algebra of Section~\ref{sec:restr} is the
toolkit through which such bounds are proved: a width lower bound is converted to a
size bound by hitting a hypothetical small refutation with a random restriction.
Theorem~\ref{thm:hard} supplies the ``still-hard sub-instance'' guarantee, and the
\emph{exactness} of Theorem~\ref{thm:restr-inv} ensures no information is lost at
the syntax--semantics interface; the remaining difficulty is the quantitative
counting of surviving wide clauses.

CDCL SAT solvers produce traces equivalent to resolution refutations on UNSAT
instances, so Theorem~\ref{thm:ref-sound} underwrites their verdicts, and Haken's
theorem explains their failure on counting problems; pseudo-Boolean solvers in the
spirit of cutting planes dispatch exactly those instances.

\section{Future work}

\begin{conjecture}[Width explosion]
Every resolution refutation of $\PHP_n$ must, at some stage, manipulate a clause
mentioning a constant fraction of all $n(n+1)$ variables. Hardness is preserved
under every partial assignment, so a refutation cannot localize its reasoning
without leaving a still-unsatisfiable sub-instance.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Random restriction collapse]
A sub-exponential refutation of $\PHP_n$, hit by a random restriction fixing most
variables, has all its wide clauses collapse, leaving a too-narrow refutation of a
still-hard sub-instance. The exact commutation of restriction and satisfaction
isolates the surviving-wide-clause count as the sole remaining difficulty.
\end{conjecture}

A third direction proposes \emph{counting capacity} --- the arithmetic
expressiveness per proof step --- as the right axis along which to measure and
separate proof systems.

\section{Conclusion}

Resolution certifies the pigeonhole principle's impossibility soundly yet, by
Haken's theorem, only at exponential cost, because it cannot count. Restrictions
--- exact, lossless, hardness-preserving --- are the engine behind such lower
bounds, and the principle stays hard under every partial decision. Cutting planes,
armed with integer arithmetic and Chv\'atal--Gomory rounding, leaps the wall in
linearly many steps, separating itself exponentially from resolution. The results
assembled here form the algebra of that story --- soundness, hardness, robustness,
and separation.

\end{document}

